  "provider_query": "The first YouTube video, Me at the zoo",
  "answer": "**“Me at the zoo”** is the first video uploaded to YouTube. Key facts and sources:\n\n1. **Uploader and date** — YouTube co-founder Jawed Karim uploaded it on April 23, 2005 (about 8:27 p.m. UTC). It is a roughly 19-second clip. [web:1]\n\n2. **Location and content** — Filmed at the San Diego Zoo in front of the elephants. Karim says they have “really, really, really long trunks, and that’s cool,” and that is “pretty much all there is to say.” [web:1] [web:9]\n\n3. **Who filmed it** — Recorded by Yakov Lapitsky. The elephants behind him have been identified as Connie and Timbo. [web:1]\n\n4. **Official video** — Still on the channel “jawed”: [youtube.com/watch?v=jNQXAC9IVRw](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jNQXAC9IVRw). Recent figures show hundreds of millions of views (on the order of 440 million) and tens of millions of likes. [web:1] [web:9]\n\n5. **Why it exists** — Karim, Chad Hurley, and Steve Chen built YouTube so video sharing would not depend on clunky plugins or downloads. Karim has said an early spark was the difficulty of finding clips of the 2004 Janet Jackson Super Bowl incident. [web:0] [web:1]\n\n6. **Was it really first?** — Claims of earlier uploads (including glitched dates or a supposed “test” video) have been widely debunked. In an interview, Karim said this was the first video: he uploaded it to a test database that was then made the live database. [web:3] [web:5]\n\n7. **Platform context** — YouTube’s domain was registered earlier in 2005; public uploads became possible around April 23, 2005. The early site had a simple layout, without comments, subscriptions, or monetization as they exist today. [web:1] [web:3]\n\n8. **Later recognition** — The San Diego Zoo has publicly noted the honor (including a high-profile comment on the video) and marked the 20th anniversary in 2025 with tributes. Google bought YouTube in 2006 for stock then valued at a little over $1 billion. [web:2] [web:4] [web:9]",
